  "body": "## What changed\n\n- The release gate of the v0.20.0 cut stopped in `step_files_agree_or_refuse_by_name` on `testdata/models/bspline_discontinuous.step`.\n- Cause: the solidcore reader and the solidcore mesher answer on that file in a few milliseconds (probe run, debug). The bench's own truck reader (`truck_read` in `crates/kernel/tests/step_util/mod.rs`) called `robust_triangulation` with no meter. truck's subdivision does not end on a B-spline that is not continuous. truck reads no cancel token. Thus the time bound had no effect, and the test thread did not stop.\n- Fix: the new file `crates/kernel/tests/step_util/truck_guard.rs` wraps each face in a metered surface. This is the bench copy of the meter that crates/app uses since #1536 (25 M evaluations for each face). The meter also reads the bench's cancel token. After the cap or the cancel, the bench refuses the file by name, for example `shell #17: face 6 of 6 (B-spline surface) not meshed: its subdivision did not converge within the evaluation cap`.\n- `truck_read` now takes the cancel token. `step_agreement.rs` and `step_import.rs` give it.\n- Three fixtures had no verdict row in `step_agreement.rs`. They now have rows: `bspline_discontinuous.step` (both refuse), `bspline_dome.step` (agree, mesh volume off: a FINDING on solidcore-tess), `fillet_corner.step` (solidcore refuses).\n- New dev-dependency: `truck-topology` (workspace pin, already in the graph through crates/app). truck-stepio does not re-export `CompressedShell`. No `camcraft-*` edge. The library is not changed. Decision: `docs/decisions/1697-bench-truck-guard.md`.\n\n## How tested (debug, Windows)\n\n- `step_agreement`: 1 passed in 10.1 s. Before the fix, the test failed at its 90 s bound.\n- `step_import`: 4 passed, with the new pinning test `truck_side_refuses_a_runaway_face_and_obeys_its_cancel`.\n- `solidcraft_hostile` 3 passed, `solidcraft_import` 7 passed.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-kernel --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- The kernel suites in release mode, and the release lane `mesh_deviation_bench` (it uses its own fixture list, and this change does not touch it).\n- Linux and macOS.\n\nChangelog: none\nEffort: high\n\nFixes #1697\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
